      "math_academy_id": "q-66792",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm",
      "problem": "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x}={2}^{x+1}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & ={2}^{x+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
