      "knowledge_point": "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases",
      "problem": "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${5}^{4x+1}={6}^{7x+5}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{4x+1} & ={6}^{7x+5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{4x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{7x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(4x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)=(7x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(7x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
